{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CgGdZAeB3nkeY18KeyqeX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MC2ZZwVdypkm8wCGHEnp3k","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"[REDACTED:local-path:2e2781ac9c86]/mnt/outputs/artifacts/deck-review-acmecorp/handoff/cw-dr-smoke-0a1b2c3d/checklist_output.json","content":"{
  "slide_reviews_read_token": "bda3c3f3baf1",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide states 'Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders,' naming a product category and ICP but providing no quantified outcome. Sequoia's framework requires the purpose sentence to deliver '[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome]' — the outcome ('cuts integration from 20+ hours/week') appears only on the problem slide, not paired with the company purpose statement.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the title slide tagline to include the quantified outcome: 'Acmecorp — the CLI that reduces developer tooling integration from 20+ hours/week to under one hour for indie SaaS founders,' so the purpose and its value are stated together in a single line."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slides 2 and 4 carry conclusion-headlines ('Problem: 20+ hours/week wasted on developer tooling integration' and 'Traction: $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth'). Slides 1, 5, and 6 use topic labels: 'Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders,' 'Team: 2 co-founders, ex-Stripe and ex-Notion,' and 'Ask: $1.0M–$2.0M seed raise.' DocSend and Sequoia recommend every headline be a conclusion so investors skimming can follow the story without reading slide body text.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the topic-label headlines to carry conclusions: slide 5 becomes 'Two ex-Stripe/Notion founders built this stack themselves' and slide 6 becomes 'We need $2.0M to reach $60K MRR and Series A readiness.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows Problem (slide 2) → Solution (slide 3) → Traction (slide 4) → Team (slide 5) → Ask (slide 6), which is a partial arc. Five slides expected in the seed framework are absent: Why Now, Market Size, Competition, Business Model, and GTM. Sequoia, DocSend, and YC all require the full Problem → Solution → Why Now → Proof → Ask arc; the current deck jumps from solution to traction without establishing market opportunity, competitive positioning, or growth motion.",
      "notes": "Insert the five missing framework slides in the expected order for seed stage: after the solution slide add Why Now, then Market Size; after traction add Competition, Business Model, and GTM before the team and ask slides."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The traction slide ($8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth over 4 months) is slide 4, within the first four slides. DocSend data shows that if a reader reaches slide 4, 82% finish the deck — placing the strongest proof point here follows best practice."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The slides are brief (word count estimates of 10–22 words each), which means each slide's headline and a single supporting fact carry the full story. Slides 1, 3, and 5 are especially thin: slide 1 is positioning without context, slide 3 lists tools without explaining the workflow change, and slide 5 lists employer names without naming founders or establishing why their background qualifies them. DocSend notes that investors spend approximately 2:30 on a shared deck — a deck with minimal body text relies on the investor to fill in the gaps these slides leave.",
      "notes": "Expand slides 3 and 5: add a 3-step before/after workflow to the solution slide and add founder names with a one-sentence founder-market-fit statement to the team slide so both slides communicate their point without verbal narration."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 states '20+ hours/week wasted on developer tooling integration' targeting 'indie SaaS founders' — a quantified time cost with a specific ICP. However, no source, customer quote, or discovery call evidence validates the figure, and no dollar cost of inaction is stated. Sequoia's problem framework requires the cost of inaction to be explicit (time, money, risk, or missed revenue), and YC recommends concrete customer quotes rather than asserted figures.",
      "notes": "Add either a customer quote or a discovery call reference to validate the '20+ hours/week' claim, and add a cost-of-inaction line: at $150/hour opportunity cost, 20 hours/week is $156,000/year — stating this makes the business case explicit."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 describes 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI' — a feature list naming the integrations without showing the workflow change. There is no before/after comparison, no product screenshot, no demo reference, and no explanation of what a founder does differently after adopting Acmecorp. Sequoia and DocSend both require the solution slide to show the workflow change and value delivered, not technical architecture or a capability list.",
      "notes": "Replace the feature list with a 3-step before/after workflow: 'Before: manually maintaining broken webhooks between GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel. After: one Acmecorp CLI command syncs all four — zero maintenance.' Add a terminal screenshot or demo link."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no Why Now slide and no slide that argues why this is the right moment to build Acmecorp. All 6 slides were checked: none addresses a macro catalyst, a new platform primitive, a behavioral shift, or the answer to Sequoia's question 'Why wasn't this built before?'",
      "notes": "Add a Why Now slide citing a specific macro catalyst: for example, the proliferation of SaaS APIs making integration maintenance harder for solo founders, the growth of the indie SaaS movement, or the emergence of AI coding tools that increase productivity while expanding integration surface area."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market sizing slide. All 6 slides were checked and none presents TAM, SAM, SOM, or any sizing methodology. The slide reviews flag 'Market Size (TAM/SAM/SOM)' as a critical missing slide. Sequoia and YC both require bottom-up market sizing at seed stage — ICP count × willingness to pay × adoption rate — not just a top-down TAM claim.",
      "notes": "Add a market sizing slide using a bottom-up approach: estimate the number of indie SaaS founders globally, multiply by willingness to pay (the current implied ARPU of approximately $333/month from $8K MRR across 24 customers is a starting point), and apply a realistic adoption rate to reach SAM and SOM."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. All 6 slides were checked and none maps competitors, incumbents, or alternatives. The slide reviews flag 'Competition / Alternatives' as a critical missing slide. DocSend data shows VCs spent 88% more time on the competition slide in successful decks versus unsuccessful ones — omitting it signals either market naivety or deliberate avoidance.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping direct competitors (other CLI tools, integration platforms like Zapier or Make for developers), incumbents (native API integrations from GitHub, Stripe, etc.), and the 'do nothing / build in-house' alternative, with 2–3 concrete differentiators for Acmecorp."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no business model slide. All 6 slides were checked and none states a pricing model, price level, gross margin, or buyer-versus-user distinction. The slide reviews note that the implied ARPU of approximately $333/month ($8K MRR / 24 customers) is never stated as a price point. Sequoia's framework requires pricing model, buyer vs. user, and gross margin structure.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide stating: pricing structure (seat-based or usage-based), target ACV per customer, whether the current $333/month implied ARPU is the intended price point or an early-adopter discount, and gross margin structure."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no GTM slide. All 6 slides were checked and none explains how Acmecorp acquires customers, what channel drove the 24 paying customers, or what the sales cycle looks like. The slide reviews flag 'Go-to-Market' as an important missing slide. Sequoia and DocSend require ICP + channel + early channel proof at seed stage.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide explaining how the first 24 customers were acquired (word-of-mouth, community, product-led growth, or another channel), which channel is most repeatable, and what the planned growth motion is post-raise."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 states 'Two co-founders: one from Stripe (engineering) and one from Notion (product)' — employer pedigree is present and signals quality, but neither founder is named and no founder-market fit is established. DocSend data shows the team slide receives approximately 46 seconds out of 2:30 total reading time; Sequoia requires the team slide to answer 'why you' with founder-market fit and complementary skills, not just employer names. Since the deck was submitted as text rather than an uploaded file, absence of photos or LinkedIn links is not scored.",
      "notes": "Add both founders' full names and a one-sentence bio explaining what each built at Stripe and Notion, then add a sentence establishing founder-market fit — for example, 'Both co-founders were indie SaaS founders themselves before building Acmecorp.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The slides that are present (Problem, Solution, Traction, Team, Ask) appear in a reasonable order consistent with the seed framework — Problem before Solution, Solution before Traction, Team near the end, Ask last. However, five of the ten expected seed-stage framework slides are absent: Why Now, Market Size, Competition, Business Model, and GTM. These gaps are scored by their respective content criteria, not here.",
      "notes": "Insert the missing framework slides in their expected positions: Why Now immediately after Solution, Market Size after Why Now, Competition and Business Model after Traction, GTM before Team."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The traction slide presents '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months)' — MRR, growth rate, and paying customer count are the expected seed-stage traction metrics. However, retention and churn data are absent and NRR is not discussed. Sequoia and a16z flag growth without retention context as a significant gap at seed stage — 18% MoM growth is a meaningful signal only when it is not masking churn.",
      "notes": "Add at minimum one retention or churn data point to the traction slide: 'all 24 customers still active' or a monthly churn rate directly addresses the leaky-bucket question an investor will raise immediately."
    },
    {
